WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.360
Hello there, and welcome to&nbsp;
this unusual AI Coffee Break.&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.760
Ms. Coffee Bean forced me&nbsp;
to relax a bit and have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:24.080
fun than we usually do when discussing&nbsp;
papers in our totally serious fashion.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:27.440
Wheeee!
Yes, serious! Totally serious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.000
we have to be serious, it’s science!
 
This video starts with a throwback to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.680
this comment of a Reddit user about one&nbsp;
of my first videos with Ms. Coffee Bean.&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.840
So someone on Reddit said that&nbsp;
I should ditch the TTS...!&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:51.440
Which means that they think I have used a TTS&nbsp;
for that video... TTS is "text to speech".&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.360
Let's hear a snippet from&nbsp;
that video, is it that bad?&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.160
"Hello there! In the last video, we&nbsp;
outlined the history of the Transformer".&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.560
Oh, I think that was the wrong audio file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.120
because it really sounded like TTS.
Ok, now with the correct audio file.&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.840
"I hope that now that you know about the&nbsp;
transformer's success and proliferation..."

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.160
Wow, I could have spoken that so much better...

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.760
But what did I know back&nbsp;
then? We were still so young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:25.840
and you were just a little Ms. Coffee Bean!
Today you’re like 2 pixels bigger, right?

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.280
But let’s stick to that TTS a little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.400
because there is a reason why we used&nbsp;
exactly the text-to-speech you heard:

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:40.080
It is the default TTS packed with&nbsp;
NVIDIA Jarvis releasing today. Yay!

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.960
So let’s hear again its output because&nbsp;
it is more to it than you first hear:&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:50.160
"And if you are not very much excited yet, this&nbsp;
is the best part of the Transformers. [Okay.]

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.000
They train very easily with self-supervision&nbsp;
[woo, cool!], meaning that they do not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.560
necessarily need annotated training data [that's&nbsp;
awesome!] to be able to capture human language."

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.520
There could be some improvement here, right?

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.520
But there are also very good parts!
We can blame the good and the bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.120
parts onto different parts that come into the TTS.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.520
And here is where NVIDIA Jarvis comes in:

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:18.880
While I was using here the default models that are&nbsp;
already trained on lots and lots of training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.960
the whole Jarvis AI is modular such that you&nbsp;
can exchange the parts you are not content with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.520
with other better ones that you&nbsp;
or someone else has trained.&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.720
And not only with better modules, but with more&nbsp;
specific ones, until you are content with it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.360
and the whole system is ready to deploy.
Like I would like to train the TTS to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.760
synthesize my voice and my thick eastern&nbsp;
European accent to speak these videos.&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.920
In accent: "And you haven't even&nbsp;
heard my eastern European accent yet!"&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.920
My ultimate goal is to make&nbsp;
Ms. Coffee Bean fully sentient!&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:02.320
But because I do not want to train those&nbsp;
better or specific modules from scratch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:08.400
NVIDIA has got me covered, since you can&nbsp;
take the existing models and fine-tune them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.720
in the Transfer Learning Toolkit on&nbsp;
your own data to make it better fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.840
your style or, you know, your&nbsp;
customers if you have any.&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.880
Then you can deploy it with Jarvis which&nbsp;
optimizes the models and makes them fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:27.040
for exactly the GPU you are using such that&nbsp;
your deployed models can work real-time.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.280
And it's really easy to set up! It only took&nbsp;
Letitia like a whole day to get it to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.400
while I sat in the coffee roaster the whole time!

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:43.120
Nah, kidding, Letitia just needed the whole&nbsp;
day to figure out that 8GB of VRAM are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.640
not enough for some of the bigger Natural&nbsp;
Language Understanding Modules of Jarvis.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.960
And while NVIDIA kindly&nbsp;
offered to provide a bigger GPU&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.720
which she did not accept,&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:01.240
they also let her know how to get it&nbsp;
to run on her measly 3070 RTX. Phew!

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.640
And afterwards it took only minutes to set up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.760
Text-to-Speech functionality&nbsp;
of Jarvis for this video.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.240
So for the Jarvis default Text-To-Speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:15.520
one does not need more than 8 GB&nbsp;
of VRAM and it's fact checked!

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.640
And the TTS is just a tiny part of NVIDIA&nbsp;
Jarvis: it comes also with Speech to text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.800
and Natural Language Understanding&nbsp;
modules that you can combine how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.600
want. Just think about the possibilities of&nbsp;
creating your own applications with that.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.880
It can help you transcribe your chat&nbsp;
with another person or tag Named Entities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.520
and answer your questions.
 
